 "seat": {
  "BEST_WHY": "The floor is genuinely cut, not tiled. The courses do not run in bond \u2014 a slab left of the figure at (200,470)\u2013(300,530) spans about three cells while the blocks at (560,600)\u2013(620,650) are near-square, and the horizontal joints step at irregular pitches (measured spacings of 30, 26, 40, 32 display px down the run at x\u2248500). Better: the long crack that enters at (300,760), forks at (325,610) and runs up to (500,510) crosses block boundaries and joints without breaking \u2014 it is drawn on the surface, not per-tile, which is the single detail that stops a stone floor reading as a grid. The top wall run at y\u2248385\u2013440 carries vertical timber pins driven at irregular intervals (x\u2248265, 345, 420, 500), each one a different length, sitting proud of the masonry with a darker underside band at y\u2248440 that separates the wall's top face from the floor below it. The light falls off monotonically and plausibly \u2014 sampled mean luminance by radius from the figure: 168 at r=24, 129 at r=72, 99 at r=120, 55 at r=192 \u2014 and the corridor at x\u2248500\u2013530 takes a shaft of that light northward and loses it by y\u2248180, which is the correct behaviour for a corridor mouth rather than a painted gradient.",
  "WORST_WHY": "Mixed projection in a frame that has to be top-down. The crates at (60,0)\u2013(96,70) and the barrels at (115,193)\u2013(145,216) and (164,193)\u2013(190,216) are drawn in three-quarter elevation \u2014 you can read the barrel staves down the side and the lit lid ellipse on top \u2014 while the ground they sit on is strict plan view. The mud-to-stone boundary at y\u2248216 is a dead-straight horizontal pixel line with no transition, no lip and no debris; the mud simply stops. Both water edges are worse: hard vertical seams at x\u2248248 and x\u2248344, full height, no bank, no shallows, no shadow under the stone lip. The mud texture repeats measurably \u2014 autocorrelation of the region x 104\u2013232, y 0\u2013192 peaks at lag 48 in both axes (0.149 vertical, 0.116 horizontal, above every neighbouring lag), so the same noise patch is stamped on a 48px lattice and you can find the same dark-red squiggle cluster three times without looking hard. And there is no light in the frame at all: luminance standard deviation is 18.3 across the whole image, uniform corner to corner, in a place that is supposed to be lit only by what the figure is carrying. The green figure at (208,0)\u2013(240,30) has a cast ellipse under its feet; nothing else in the frame casts anything.",
